 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: &quot;Red undertow&quot; threatens GOP midterm map 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump told voters to pretend he's on the ballot. Apparently they're listening:

 With midterms 38 days away, Republicans coast to coast are getting pulled under by a violent undertow fueled by Trump's unpopularity, Axios' Marc Caputo, Hans Nichols and Alex Isenstadt report. Why it matters: It's looked bad for Republicans all year, but this week felt different — like the political environment was disintegrating in real time, one ominous data point after another.

 Most worrisome for the GOP, there's virtually no chance the war ends, inflation cools, interest rates fall, consumer confidence rises, and economic optimism surges before the Nov. 3 election. 💡 Lingo du jour: Cook Political Report founder Charlie Cook writes that while "many have long predicted that Democrats will enjoy a 'blue wave' election, I think of it more as a 'red undertow,' which will pull many Republicans under and significantly trim the margins for many others."

 That's an implied reminder that Democrats' favorability ratings aren't good. They're just better than those of Republicans. 👂 Start with mood music: This week, Trump's disapproval rating hit 60.5%, the worst mark of his two terms in office, including the aftermath of the Jan. 6 riots.

 Gas prices rose to $4.49 a gallon , diesel has hit $6.50 and no relief is in sight. Seven months in, the U.S. is locked in a standoff with Iran with no apparent exit strategy. Grocery prices are still rising year over year. Mortgage rates hit 7%, and the cost of borrowing across the economy continues to climb. 🗳️ Enter the elections: The problems for the GOP are everywhere. In the battle for the Senate, once ruby-red Texas looks more in play than ever, and Florida isn't far behind. Nor is Alaska. North Carolina and Georgia look lost. Maine is problematic.

 The Senate GOP campaign arm is begging donors for another $8.9 million to defend Senate seats in three states that Trump won by double digits two years ago — Alaska, Iowa and Ohio. "We're having problems just getting Republican voters to respond to polls," a national Republican pollster told us. "At what point do we think these guys aren't showing up at the actual polls on Election Day?" Only in Michigan do Republicans see glimmers of hope to pick up a Senate seat because Democratic nominee Abdul El-Sayed is seen by some as so radical that he has "scared the shit out of our people to want to vote," the GOP pollster said. "But even then, in this environment, who knows?"

 What once looked like a longshot for Democrats — taking control of the Senate by flipping four Republican-held seats on a map favoring the GOP — looks increasingly plausible. ⚡ On the House side, Cook Political Report moved 15 House races toward Democrats yesterday. They need to net just three seats to win control of the chamber.

 Republicans are now playing defense deep in red America. GOP-aligned groups are spending money in more than a half-dozen districts that Trump won by at least 15 points in 2024. State of play: More and more lately, Republican incumbents are running for cover from Trump.

 In Iowa, two House Republicans have switched their positions to support a war powers resolution on Iran. Sen. Chuck Grassley, who rode the 1980 Republican wave to the Senate, and Rep. Ashley Hinson, who's running for Iowa's other Senate seat, are demanding restrictions on diesel exports. 💰 The other side: A Trump adviser downplayed the GOP tremors, pointing to the Republicans' massive cash advantage.

 "If we execute and our people show up, it's a different ballgame," the adviser said. "There's more than a month to go." Share this story . 

 
 
