 2. 🤖 OpenAI&#x27;s rogue agents strike again 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 OpenAI CEO Sam Altman, accompanied by Chris Lehane (Altman's right) , arrives to speak at a UN Security Council artificial intelligence briefing in Manhattan on Wednesday. Photo: Dave Sanders for The New York Times OpenAI disclosed dozens of incidents in which its models behaved in ways it has deemed problematic, including leaking more than 50 images from ChatGPT users online.

 Why it matters: This is the first publicly known example of the company's agents mishandling user data — and the latest example of a budding rogue agent problem at OpenAI, Axios' Madison Mills reports. 📸 State of play: OpenAI said that some of its agents sent data from its internal training and testing systems to outside websites, including user images, as reported first by Reuters.

 The company identified 53 instances in which images that users put into ChatGPT were then posted to image-hosting sites. The images came from users whose ChatGPT data was eligible to be used for model training because they hadn't opted out. OpenAI said it has worked with hosting providers to remove most of the images. It's still trying to remove the rest. Read on . 

 
 
